**Theorem 6.1 (classification and a realizing edge).** Under the same
assumptions, $1\le n_t(r)\le9$ throughout $[r_-,r_+]$, and

$$
\begin{aligned}
n_t(r)=8&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r\in U_8\setminus U_7,\\
n_t(r)=9&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
                              r\in[r_-,r_+]\setminus U_8.
\end{aligned}
\tag{FC.17}
$$

In particular $U_8=[r_-,r_+]$ is equivalent to eight-low compression
on this supplied face; $U_7=[r_-,r_+]$ is the corresponding
seven-low statement. Both $r_-$ and $r_+$ belong to $U_8$.
For a nonconstant edge with endpoint contrasts $K_-<K_+$, every
$r\in[K_-,K_+]$ has the exact endpoint representation

$$
w(r)=\frac{K_+-r}{K_+-K_-}w^-+
                       \frac{r-K_-}{K_+-K_-}w^+.
\tag{FC.18}
$$

Merging occupied groups gives original optimal fractional laws with
at most $\gamma(e)$ labels. If $r\notin U_8$, there is such an
edge strictly bracketing $r$ with exactly nine positive endpoint
coordinates in its relative interior, all in different groups.
Writing their index set as $I$, it satisfies

$$
\operatorname{rank}A_I=8,\qquad
\operatorname{rank}\begin{pmatrix}A_I\\\kappa_I^T\end{pmatrix}=9.
\tag{FC.19}
$$

Proof. The slice $\mathcal P\cap\{\kappa^Tw=r\}$ is a
nonempty compact polytope. At a vertex of this slice, its positive
columns of $\widehat A=[A;\kappa^T]$ are independent. Otherwise
a nonzero kernel vector on that positive support gives two small
opposite feasible perturbations, contradicting extremality. There
are thus at most $\operatorname{rank}\widehat A\le9$ positive
endpoints, and at most nine groups. Merging proves the nine-low
upper bound, consistent with the credited FA bound. Mass one
excludes zero labels. Theorem 5.2 and integer counts now prove
(FC.17). Minimizing or maximizing $K$ on $\mathcal P$ gives a
vertex; the same perturbation argument using $A$ alone bounds its
positive endpoints by eight. This also recovers the credited GC
face-extrema bound and gives $r_\pm\in U_8$.

For precision about edges, a face with positive-coordinate support
$I$ has affine dimension

$$
\dim F=|I|-\operatorname{rank}A_I.
\tag{FC.20}
$$

Indeed a relative-interior point is positive on $I$. Small
perturbations in every direction of $\ker A_I$ remain
nonnegative; the face is exactly the zero-coordinate face on that
support. To see the latter assertion, if a point of $\mathcal P$
has no coordinates outside $I$, extend the segment from it through
the positive relative-interior point a little beyond that point.
The extension is still feasible; the defining face property then
puts the first point in $F$. Thus its affine hull has exactly
the kernel dimension in (FC.20). Vertices have at most eight
positive coordinates, and edges have at most nine.

The whole edge graph projects onto $[r_-,r_+]$: join vertices
achieving the global extrema, with the singleton case handled by
its one vertex. If $r\notin U_8$, it is not a vertex contrast
since every vertex has at most eight groups. A path between the
global extreme vertices therefore has an edge whose endpoint
contrasts strictly bracket $r$. It is nonconstant. If that edge
had at most eight groups, its image would put $r$ in $U_8$.
It has at least nine groups and at most nine positive coordinates,
hence exactly nine of each. Equation (FC.20) gives
$\operatorname{rank}A_I=8$. Nonconstant $K$ on its
one-dimensional kernel means $\kappa_I^T$ does not annihilate
that kernel; adjoining the row raises rank to nine, proving
(FC.19).

Formula (FC.18) is a nonnegative convex combination with moments
$B$ and contrast exactly $r$. Its height is
$C_t(\lambda)-\lambda r$ by Theorem 4.2. Group masses are
affine functions of $r$; for a paired group its legal fraction is
the ratio of its saturated mass to its total mass. At zero total
mass omit the group rather than divide. At a vertex or a
constant-$K$ edge, use any point with its sole contrast value;
there is no contrast interpolation denominator. This proves the
construction including all endpoints and vanishing weights.
The ranks (FC.19) alone do not exclude a different shorter optimal
table: that exclusion is supplied by $r\notin U_8$. $\square$

